  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Fitness executive Chris Rondeau was prepared to abandon an expensive project when it failed franchisees. Planet Fitness had developed its own payment and sales software, but the system proved unreliable. He decided to stop the project despite the money already spent, treating dependable service to franchise operators as more important than preserving the investment.\n\nSeparating senior management from other employees proved another experiment worth reversing. An offsite executive office was intended to give staff more independence and leaders fewer interruptions. Instead, decisions slowed and relationships weakened. Rondeau favoured remaining accessible, visiting different departments and hearing employees describe their work in person.",
    "html": "<p>Fitness executive Chris Rondeau was prepared to abandon an expensive project when it failed franchisees. Planet Fitness had developed its own payment and sales software, but the system proved unreliable. He decided to stop the project despite the money already spent, treating dependable service to franchise operators as more important than preserving the investment.</p>\r\n<p>Separating senior management from other employees proved another experiment worth reversing. An offsite executive office was intended to give staff more independence and leaders fewer interruptions. Instead, decisions slowed and relationships weakened. Rondeau favoured remaining accessible, visiting different departments and hearing employees describe their work in person.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 101,
